ould have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Yuma has set <strong class=\"sig\">2 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 66 that chance alone would give, and <strong>98 record highs</strong>, far more than the 65 that chance alone would give. The 1950s hold the most record highs of any decade (84). Yuma's all-time high is 124\u00b0F, on July 28, 1995.","t_chg":"Yuma's days and nights both warmed. Nights warmed faster.","p_chg":"Across 140 complete years: Yuma's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.50\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.45 to +0.56), about +7.3\u00b0F since 1879. Yuma's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.20\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.15 to +0.25), about +2.9\u00b0F since 1879.","t_mon":"Lows rose in all 12 months. Highs rose in all 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Yuma's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (\u22120.01 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 3.0 inches a year. The wettest year was 1926 (9.23 in); the driest, 2002 (0.03 in). Yuma almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.0 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 15 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}